From d6b77261b3d9c71053dce095abfc67075e4d5944 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jacob Root <me@rooty.dev>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 06:09:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/11] devices: virtio-nvgpu's UVM aperture, in the window's
 BAR

A virtio-pci device can now publish more than one shared memory region:
VirtioDevice::get_extra_shared_memory_regions (empty by default, so no other
device changes) lists regions published after the one
get_shared_memory_region returns, in the same 64-bit prefetchable BAR, each
with its own VIRTIO_PCI_CAP_SHARED_MEMORY_CFG capability at its own offset
(shared_memory_bar_layout: each region on a boundary of its own size, at most
1 GiB; one region is laid out exactly as before). The guest finds regions by
id (virtio_get_shm_region), so its driver needs nothing new. The transport
reports the layout it allocated -- from the main process, before the device
is moved into a process of its own -- for the main process to hold the
device's requests to (vm_control's SharedMemoryBarLayout), with the queue
notification addresses of its settings BAR for the ioevent tube (no more
than its 4 KiB notification area, whatever queue count the backend reports).

The nvgpu vhost-user frontend publishes region 2, the UVM aperture (at most
1 GiB), after the window when the backend's GET_SHMEM_CONFIG reports it,
which vhost-user-nvgpu does only with --allow-compute. Regions are taken by
id however many the backend reports: a lone region that is not region 1 is
no window. Its backend request handler keeps a record per region and checks
a SHMEM_MAP on the aperture as a UVM semaphore pool first -- shared
read-write, a whole number of pages up to 64 MiB, at a host address (its
file offset) in [4 GiB, 32 TiB), at an aperture offset on the backend's
2 MiB boundary, clear of every live pool in the aperture and in host
memory, at most 64 pools and 256 MiB -- and passes it to the mapper as a
pool (SharedMemoryMapper::add_uvm_pool_mapping, whose default refuses),
which VmRequester sends to the main process as RegisterUvmPool. A reset
withdraws pools as it does window mappings.

The main process holds virtio-nvgpu's shared memory and ioevent tubes to
what the device needs (vm_control::sys::linux::nvgpu), withdraws what a
device placed when its tube goes away, and, when a vhost-user device is of
type nvgpu, reserves the UVM pool band [4 GiB, 32 TiB) at the start of
run_config and finds nvidia-uvm's major in /proc/devices.

